---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import PythonWorkflow from '../../../components/tutorials/PythonWorkflow';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Simulation with CasADi"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Code Generation Workflows', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Simulation with CasADi' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Circuit Components', href: '/tutorials/visualization/circuit-components' }}
  next={{ label: 'SymPy & Bode Plots', href: '/tutorials/workflows/sympy-bode' }}
>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Rumoca can compile Modelica models directly into CasADi symbolic expressions. This gives you
    a fully symbolic ODE representation that integrates naturally with CasADi's ecosystem for
    simulation, sensitivity analysis, and optimization. Instead of manually transcribing equations,
    you define your dynamics once in Modelica and let the compiler produce the CasADi code.
  </p>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">
    Try compiling the mass-spring-damper model below. The CasADi backend generates Python code
    that defines the state vector, parameters, and ODE right-hand side as symbolic expressions.
    The compiled output is automatically combined with simulation and plotting code into a
    complete, runnable script that you can also open in Google Colab.
  </p>

  <PythonWorkflow
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model MassSpringDamperControl
  Real x(start = 1) "Position";
  Real v(start = 0) "Velocity";
  input Real u "Control force";
  parameter Real m = 1.0 "Mass";
  parameter Real k = 1.0 "Spring stiffness";
  parameter Real c = 0.5 "Damping";
equation
  der(x) = v;
  m * der(v) = -k * x - c * v + u;
end MassSpringDamperControl;`}
    modelName="MassSpringDamperControl"
    backend="casadi"
    pipInstalls={['casadi', 'matplotlib', 'numpy']}
    title="CasADi Simulation Script"
    pythonTemplate={`{generated_code}

import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Build the integrator
dae = {"x": x, "p": ca.vertcat(u, p), "ode": f}
integrator = ca.integrator("sim", "cvodes", dae, 0, np.linspace(0, 10, 200))

# Set initial state and parameters
x0 = [1.0, 0.0]          # position=1, velocity=0
p_val = [0.0,             # u  (control force = 0)
         1.0, 1.0, 0.5]   # m, k, c

# Run the simulation
result = integrator(x0=x0, p=p_val)
x_traj = np.array(result["xf"])

# Plot the results
t = np.linspace(0, 10, 200)
plt.plot(t, x_traj[0, :], label="position")
plt.plot(t, x_traj[1, :], label="velocity")
plt.xlabel("Time [s]")
plt.legend()
plt.title("Mass-Spring-Damper Free Response")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()`}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-12 mb-4">Generated Code Structure</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The generated CasADi code defines three key objects:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6 space-y-2">
    <li>
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">x</code> &mdash; the state vector, containing
      all continuous states (here, position and velocity).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">p</code> &mdash; the parameter vector, containing
      all model parameters (mass, spring stiffness, damping).
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">f</code> &mdash; the ODE function, a CasADi
      symbolic expression representing <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">dx/dt = f(x, u, p)</code>.
      This is the right-hand side of the differential equations.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-8 mb-4">Next Steps</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
    This CasADi workflow is the foundation for more advanced use cases. Because the generated code
    is fully symbolic, CasADi can compute exact derivatives automatically. This makes it
    straightforward to move from simulation to optimization. The
    <a href="/tutorials/optimization/optimal-control" class="text-teal hover:text-accent underline">optimal control tutorials</a>
    build directly on this pattern, using the same generated ODE to formulate and solve
    trajectory optimization and MPC problems.
  </p>
</TutorialLayout>
